<UserControl xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
             xmlns:d="http://schemas.microsoft.com/expression/blend/2008"
             xmlns:mc="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/markup-compatibility/2006"
             xmlns:controls="clr-namespace:KinesisEdit.Controls"
             xmlns:views="clr-namespace:KinesisEdit.Views"
             xmlns:vm="clr-namespace:KinesisEdit.ViewModels"
             mc:Ignorable="d" d:DesignWidth="480" d:DesignHeight="760"
             x:Class="KinesisEdit.Views.MacroInspectorPanelView"
             x:DataType="vm:MacroInspectorPanelViewModel">
  <!--
    The key inspector's Macro panel — "selecting a key edits its macro right here". It is the app's
    ONE macro surface since issue #140; there is no second surface for any of it.

    ITS LAYOUT IS THE "STANDING COMPOSE BAR" MOCK'S (issue #146), not 2i's. Four things moved at
    once and they are one idea: A SLOT IS ONLY A SLOT. It has no name and no preview — it is nothing
    but "which of this key's macros am I editing" — so the dot strip and its captioned dropdown
    collapse into a row of numbered chips, and the co-trigger latches sit on the SAME row, because
    the co-triggers are what actually tell two slots apart (06 §5). Below that the step rows lose
    their numbers, the list takes a FIXED height and scrolls, and the composer is pinned directly
    under it — always the same shape in the same place, which is what makes it usable as the one
    control that edits a step.

    THE RAIL IS 480px WIDE WHILE THIS PANEL IS SHOWING. The rail sets it off the panel's own
    WantsWideRail, so this file names no width at all.

    THERE IS NO ASSIGN BUTTON. Editing in place means the first recorded keystroke IS the macro —
    the view model creates it and puts it in the key's slot under edit.

    THERE IS NO NAME FIELD (issue #146). A macro's name is not editable in this app any more; a
    stored `macro_name_*` still round-trips through the save. The dropdown that named a slot went
    with it, and its wording survives as each chip's ToolTip.Tip — a bare numeral is not accessible
    text.

    NOTHING HERE IS A GLYPH STANDING IN FOR AN ICON. The record dots are Ellipses, the drag grip is
    `IconDragHandle` and the insert affordance `IconInsert`: U+25CF, U+283F and U+FF0B are in
    neither embedded IBM Plex family and would tofu. The rows' `×` (U+00D7), the meter join's `·`
    (U+00B7), the stepper's `+`/`−` and the modifier marks (in the `.keySymbol` face) ARE
    characters.

    MONO IS THE FILE'S VOICE. The step tokens, the modifier codes, the millisecond counts and the
    meter readings are values that exist verbatim in a config file or are counted against one;
    every sentence the app says in its own voice — and a slot ordinal, which is this app counting —
    is sans.

    THE ROOT IS A GRID SO THE DRAG HAS SOMEWHERE TO DRAW. The panel itself is still the stack it
    always was; the second child is a Canvas that holds the carried row while a drag is in flight
    (issue #128). A Canvas measures to nothing, so it costs the stack no height, and it is the LAST
    child so the ghost draws over the list rather than under it.

    ONE COMPOSER, ALWAYS ON SCREEN (issue #139). There is one block below the list that edits THE
    SELECTED STEP, and it is drawn whether or not there is one: with nothing selected every control
    inside it is disabled rather than absent, which is a deliberate exception to the design's
    "absent features are not shown" and is spelled out at that block.
  -->

  <UserControl.Resources>
    <!-- One held modifier, drawn. TWO call sites — the step row's key chip and the ghost that
         copies it — so it is a resource rather than two copies.

         The tooltip is not decoration: only a RIGHT modifier spells its side, so a bare mark is
         worn by the left one and the generic one alike and the row cannot tell them apart.
         `Description` is where the distinction the file really carries stays reachable.

         UP TO TWO RUNS, IN TWO FACES. The key-symbol family is a 19-codepoint subset with NO Latin
         letters, so the `R` side letter stays in the ordinary mono face and only the mark goes in
         `.keySymbol`; a whole `R⇧` set in that class would fall back to a system font for its `R`.
         `MacroModifierMarks.Split` hands the two out separately for exactly this.

         NEITHER RUN CARRIES A COLOUR SINCE ISSUE #146. Both used to be `muted`, which was right
         while the marks sat loose in the row beside a muted action word; they are inside
         `Border.macroStepToken` now, which sets `TextElement.Foreground` to `MacroStepKeyBrush` so
         the mark and the token it qualifies read as ONE keystroke (`⌃2`) in one hue. A `muted` here
         would override the inherited tint on one of the two runs and split the chip in half. -->
    <DataTemplate x:Key="MacroModifierMarkRun" DataType="vm:MacroModifierMarks+Mark">
      <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal"
                  VerticalAlignment="Center"
                  ToolTip.Tip="{Binding Description}">
        <TextBlock Text="{Binding Side}"
                   Classes="monoValueSmall"
                   IsVisible="{Binding HasSide}"
                   VerticalAlignment="Center" />
        <TextBlock Text="{Binding Symbol}"
                   Classes="keySymbol"
                   VerticalAlignment="Center" />
      </StackPanel>
    </DataTemplate>

    <!-- ONE TEMPLATE, TWO CALL SITES: the row in the list and the ghost carried above it. The
         ghost has to be a copy of the row, and a second hand-written copy of it is a copy that
         drifts. Nothing in here binds past the step — the row's commands are anchored on the view
         outside it — so the same template renders correctly in both places.

         IT IS THE WHOLE ROW BODY, AND THAT IS A DECISION (issue #146). The mock draws the delay as
         its own right-hand column, flush with the `×` above and below it, which would put it
         OUTSIDE the selecting button — and then the drag ghost, which renders this template alone,
         would carry a step without the delay folded behind it. A step moves with its delay
         (`MoveStep`'s rule), so a carried row that dropped its `80 ms` would be showing the user
         something other than what the release is about to move. Making the template a Grid with a
         `*` spacer buys both: the pill is right-aligned in the row because the button stretches
         (`HorizontalContentAlignment`), and the ghost is honest because the pill is inside it.

         Three runs and no fourth: the key chip, the action word, the delay. THE NUMBER IS GONE — a
         step is identified by the key it strikes, and nothing else in the app cites its ordinal. -->
    <DataTemplate x:Key="MacroStepCaption" DataType="vm:MacroInspectorStepViewModel">
      <Grid ColumnDefinitions="Auto,Auto,*,Auto" ColumnSpacing="8">
        <!-- THE KEY THAT WAS STRUCK, AS A CHIP. `MacroStepKeyBrush` is the macro badge's own
             orchid, darkened on light for type — purple already means "macro" in this app, so the
             cap's dot and this token say one thing in one hue, and the row invents no fifth
             meaning. Deliberately not the accent: that has exactly two meanings and a key token is
             neither.

             The marks are INSIDE the chip with the token (`⌃2`), because a chord is one keystroke
             and drawing `⌃` loose beside `2` read as two. The colour is set once, on the chip
             (`Border.macroStepToken`), and inherited by both runs; see `MacroModifierMarkRun`.

             Hidden entirely on a delay-only row (06 §2.2), where `HasToken` is false: there is no
             key there to draw a box around. -->
        <Border Grid.Column="0"
                Classes="macroStepToken"
                IsVisible="{Binding HasToken}"
                VerticalAlignment="Center">
          <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="3" VerticalAlignment="Center">
            <!-- 2i's marks — `⇧`, `R⌃` — and NOT the file's `LS`, `LC`, `S ` codes, which are what
                 goes on the drive rather than what a person reads (`Keystroke.FormatModifiers`,
                 untouched). -->
            <ItemsControl ItemsSource="{Binding Modifiers}"
                          ItemTemplate="{StaticResource MacroModifierMarkRun}"
                          IsVisible="{Binding HasModifiers}"
                          VerticalAlignment="Center">
              <ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
                <ItemsPanelTemplate>
                  <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="3" />
                </ItemsPanelTemplate>
              </ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
            </ItemsControl>

            <TextBlock Text="{Binding TokenText}"
                       Classes="monoValue"
                       TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis"
                       VerticalAlignment="Center" />
          </StackPanel>
        </Border>

        <TextBlock Grid.Column="1"
                   Text="{Binding ActionText}"
                   Classes="body muted"
                   VerticalAlignment="Center" />

        <!-- The delay behind the step, at the right of the row. An em dash where there is none —
             `—` is Latin-1 and both embedded families carry it — and an accent pill where there is
             one, because a delay is something the user PUT there and "you changed this" is one of
             the accent's two sanctioned meanings. The count is mono: `{d080}` is what the file
             holds (06 §2.2). Neither run carries a colour class; the pill sets
             `TextElement.Foreground` and the dash is ordinary muted type. -->
        <TextBlock Grid.Column="3"
                   Text="—"
                   Classes="meta muted"
                   IsVisible="{Binding !HasDelay}"
                   VerticalAlignment="Center" />

        <Border Grid.Column="3"
                Classes="macroStepDelay"
                IsVisible="{Binding HasDelay}"
                VerticalAlignment="Center">
          <TextBlock Text="{Binding DelayText}"
                     Classes="monoValueSmall"
                     VerticalAlignment="Center" />
        </Border>
      </Grid>
    </DataTemplate>
  </UserControl.Resources>

  <UserControl.Styles>
    <!-- ===== The drag, seen ==================================================================
         Two states and no accent between them. The accent has exactly two sanctioned meanings in
         this app — selection/focus, and "you changed this" — and "this row is being carried" is
         neither, so the carry reads as LIFT AND DIM: the source row recedes and a copy of it rides
         above the list on the popover elevation. The drop ring stays the accent's, because that
         one really is about where a selection lands.

         NOTHING HERE ANIMATES, deliberately. The ghost is pinned to the pointer every move; a
         transition on its position would make it lag the very gesture it exists to show, which is
         the complaint this feature answers. Nothing is added to Themes/Motion.axaml because
         nothing moves on a clock. -->
    <Style Selector="Grid.dragSource">
      <Setter Property="Opacity" Value="0.35" />
    </Style>

    <Style Selector="Border.macroStepGhost">
      <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource SurfaceRaisedBrush}" />
      <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource SurfaceBorderRaisedBrush}" />
      <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1" />
      <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{DynamicResource RadiusControl}" />
      <Setter Property="BoxShadow" Value="{DynamicResource ShadowPopover}" />
      <Setter Property="Padding" Value="6,4" />
    </Style>
  </UserControl.Styles>

  <Grid>
    <StackPanel>

    <!-- ===== The refusal =====================================================================
         Drawn INSTEAD of the editor, never beside it. A device with no macros, or a position that
         can never carry one (05 §5.3, spec 02's verbatim wording), is the design's sanctioned
         exception to "absent features are not shown": the user pressed this tab, and a tab that
         rendered nothing would look broken. Amber, never red — nothing failed. `pickerAdvisory` is
         the app's one amber inline block; this panel used to inline its three setters twice over. -->
    <Border Classes="inspectorSection" IsVisible="{Binding !IsAvailable}">
      <Border Classes="pickerAdvisory">
        <TextBlock Text="{Binding UnavailableReason}"
                   Classes="body statusWarning"
                   TextWrapping="Wrap" />
      </Border>
    </Border>

    <StackPanel IsVisible="{Binding IsAvailable}">

      <!-- ===== SLOTS and TRIGGER, on one row =================================================
           WHICH OF THE KEY'S MACROS IS BEING EDITED, and WHAT THE KEY HAS TO BE PRESSED WITH. A key
           holds up to five macros told apart by their co-triggers (06 §1, §5) — which is exactly
           why the two halves share a row since issue #146: reading the co-triggers three sections
           below the slot they distinguish was the panel asking the user to hold two things in their
           head.

           ONE CHIP PER SLOT THE DIALECT WRITES, never the five the model owns:
           `PersistedSlotsPerKey` is 3 on the Advantage2 and Freestyle Edge/Pro and 5 on the RGB
           family, and a macro put in slot 4 of a Freestyle Edge is gone at the next save. An
           occupied slot wears its NUMBER and an empty one a `+`, so "occupied" is said by the
           caption rather than by a fill; the accent ring says which one is under edit.

           NO `ACTIVE` BADGE AND NO `Make active`. The strip is a pure EDITING selector — every
           populated slot is live on the keyboard, and what tells them apart is the trigger latches
           beside it. Picking a slot writes `KeyboardKey.ActiveMacroIndex`, which is in-memory only
           and never serialized (05 §1.3), so it deliberately does NOT dirty the session.

           THREE LATCHES, NOT SIX AND NOT FOUR — a deliberate deviation recorded in
           docs/app/design-system.md. spec 10 pins the legacy set at "six co-trigger buttons —
           Left/Right Shift, Ctrl, Alt"; authoring here is left-only, and a right-hand or generic
           co-trigger a FILE carries lights the matching left latch instead of disappearing. There
           is no `⌘` latch: no co-trigger in specs 06 or 10 names one, so it would author a token no
           firmware is documented to honour.

           THE TRIGGER TOKEN IS GONE FROM THE STRIP (issue #146). The rail's own header already
           names the position, and drawing `hk7` twice on one rail said nothing the second time.

           ABSENT, NEVER DISABLED: a flat-list board (the Advantage 360) and a position that refuses
           macros (05 §5.3) draw no slot half at all, and a device whose dialect has no co-trigger
           draws no trigger half. The two are gated independently. -->
      <Border Classes="inspectorSection">
        <StackPanel Spacing="6">
          <Grid ColumnDefinitions="Auto,Auto,*,Auto,Auto,Auto" ColumnSpacing="6">
            <TextBlock Grid.Column="0"
                       Text="{x:Static vm:MacroInspectorPanelViewModel.SlotSectionLabel}"
                       Classes="sectionLabel"
                       IsVisible="{Binding HasSlotSelector}"
                       VerticalAlignment="Center" />

            <ItemsControl Grid.Column="1"
                          ItemsSource="{Binding SlotOptions}"
                          IsVisible="{Binding HasSlotSelector}"
                          VerticalAlignment="Center">
              <ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
                <ItemsPanelTemplate>
                  <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="4" />
                </ItemsPanelTemplate>
              </ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
              <ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
                <DataTemplate DataType="vm:MacroSlotOption">
                  <!-- Sans, not mono: a slot ordinal is this app counting, not a value that exists
                       verbatim in a config file. The caption is the tooltip, because a bare numeral
                       is not accessible text. The ancestor binding is anchored on the VIEW: a
                       `$parent[...]` written inside a themed button's content does not reach past
                       the presenter. -->
                  <Button Classes="macroChip"
                          Classes.selected="{Binding IsSelected}"
                          Classes.colliding="{Binding IsColliding}"
                          Content="{Binding ChipText}"
                          ToolTip.Tip="{Binding Caption}"
                          Command="{Binding $parent[views:MacroInspectorPanelView].((vm:MacroInspectorPanelViewModel)DataContext).SelectSlotCommand}"
                          CommandParameter="{Binding}" />
                </DataTemplate>
              </ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
            </ItemsControl>

            <TextBlock Grid.Column="3"
                       Text="{x:Static vm:MacroInspectorPanelViewModel.TriggerSectionLabel}"
                       Classes="sectionLabel"
                       IsVisible="{Binding HasCoTriggers}"
                       VerticalAlignment="Center" />

            <TextBlock Grid.Column="4"
                       Text="{x:Static vm:MacroInspectorPanelViewModel.TriggerJoin}"
                       Classes="sectionLabel"
                       IsVisible="{Binding HasCoTriggers}"
                       VerticalAlignment="Center" />

            <ItemsControl Grid.Column="5"
                          ItemsSource="{Binding CoTriggers}"
                          IsVisible="{Binding HasCoTriggers}"
                          VerticalAlignment="Center">
              <ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
                <ItemsPanelTemplate>
                  <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="4" />
                </ItemsPanelTemplate>
              </ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
              <ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
                <DataTemplate DataType="vm:MacroCoTriggerViewModel">
                  <!-- `coTriggerChip` AND NOT `macroChip` SINCE ISSUE #150, and the difference is
                       one face. The mock draws the selected TRIGGER latch as a solid accent FILL
                       where the selected slot chip and the selected composer modifier are accent
                       OUTLINES over the wash: a slot chip says "this is the one I am editing", and
                       a co-trigger says "this modifier is held", which is the app's filled-latch
                       statement everywhere else. `CoTriggerChip` is `MacroChip` with that one
                       override; everything else about the chip, including the `.colliding` amber
                       ring, is unchanged.

                       EACH LATCH IS ONE OR TWO RUNS IN TWO FACES, exactly as the step rows draw
                       them, and NEITHER RUN CARRIES A COLOUR: the theme's `.selected` face sets
                       Foreground, a TextBlock inherits it and an `Icon` (painting from Stroke)
                       cannot, which is why this site is type. -->
                  <Button Classes="coTriggerChip"
                          Classes.selected="{Binding IsOn}"
                          ToolTip.Tip="{Binding Caption}"
                          Command="{Binding $parent[views:MacroInspectorPanelView].((vm:MacroInspectorPanelViewModel)DataContext).ToggleCoTriggerCommand}"
                          CommandParameter="{Binding}">
                    <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" VerticalAlignment="Center">
                      <TextBlock Text="{Binding Side}"
                                 Classes="monoValueSmall"
                                 IsVisible="{Binding HasSide}"
                                 VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                      <TextBlock Text="{Binding Symbol}"
                                 Classes="keySymbol"
                                 IsVisible="{Binding HasMark}"
                                 VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                      <TextBlock Text="{Binding Caption}"
                                 IsVisible="{Binding !HasMark}"
                                 VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                    </StackPanel>
                  </Button>
                </DataTemplate>
              </ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
            </ItemsControl>
          </Grid>

          <!-- THE STATUS IS AN ADVISORY, AND SINCE #146 IT IS DRAWN ONLY WHEN THERE IS ONE. At rest
               the mock says nothing, because "bare press · no collision" was the panel restating
               what the empty latches already showed. A collision or a reserved trigger draws one
               amber line here AND rings every slot chip involved, so the pair is visible on the
               strip as well as named in words. Amber, never red — a collision is what `Validate()`
               reports as `MacroTriggerCollision`, and it reports rather than blocks. -->
          <TextBlock Text="{Binding TriggerStatus}"
                     Classes="meta statusWarning"
                     IsVisible="{Binding IsTriggerAdvisory}"
                     TextWrapping="Wrap" />
        </StackPanel>
      </Border>

      <!-- ===== The sequence ==================================================================
           Headed `Sequence`, with the step count where 2i's `drag ⠿ · ⌥↑↓` hint used to be and
           `● Record sequence` beside it. The hint went because the count is the thing a user wants
           from that spot; the reorder affordance is still written down, in the grip's own tooltip,
           which is now the only place the `⌥↑↓` shortcut is spelled. -->
      <Border Classes="inspectorSection">
        <StackPanel Spacing="8">
          <Grid ColumnDefinitions="Auto,Auto,*,Auto" ColumnSpacing="6">
            <TextBlock Grid.Column="0"
                       Text="{x:Static vm:MacroInspectorStepsViewModel.SectionTitle}"
                       Classes="cardTitle"
                       VerticalAlignment="Center" />

            <TextBlock Grid.Column="1"
                       Text="{Binding StepCountText}"
                       Classes="meta muted"
                       VerticalAlignment="Center" />

            <Button Grid.Column="3"
                    Classes="recordAction"
                    Command="{Binding RecordCommand}"
                    VerticalAlignment="Center">
              <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="6" VerticalAlignment="Center">
                <Ellipse Classes="recordDot" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                <!-- The 1.4s pulse (DurationRecordingPulse), reached by the class the listening cap
                     and the Tap & hold panel already share. Bound to the RUN arm alone and not to
                     `IsRecording`, which is true for either of the panel's two arms since #139: a
                     header pulsing because the composer below is armed would be offering to stop a
                     take nobody started, and its caption (RecordCommandCaption) already follows the
                     run arm for that reason. -->
                <TextBlock Text="{Binding RecordCommandCaption}"
                           Classes.recording="{Binding CaptureMode, Converter={StaticResource EnumMatch}, ConverterParameter=Run}"
                           VerticalAlignment="Center" />
              </StackPanel>
            </Button>
          </Grid>

          <!-- The live banner. Amber: it is the app telling the user their keyboard has been taken
               over, not an error — and it no longer names a step number, because the rows do not
               carry one to name.

               THE OS-RESERVED NOTE IS ITS SECOND LINE SINCE #146. It used to stand under the
               capture rule at all times, which spent two lines of the rail restating a limitation
               nobody had met yet; it belongs at the moment the limitation is discovered, which is
               while the take is running. A chord the window system keeps is consumed ABOVE the
               application and is never delivered here at all, so the sentence points at the
               composer below rather than apologising. `CaptureRule` went with the paragraph: the
               banner already says what it said. -->
          <Border Classes="pickerAdvisory" IsVisible="{Binding IsRecording}">
            <StackPanel Spacing="5">
              <TextBlock Text="{Binding RecordingBanner}" Classes="body statusWarning" TextWrapping="Wrap" />
              <TextBlock Text="{x:Static vm:MacroInspectorPanelViewModel.OsReservedNote}"
                         Classes="meta statusWarning"
                         TextWrapping="Wrap" />
            </StackPanel>
          </Border>

          <!-- The rows, in a FIXED-HEIGHT scroller (issue #146).

               AN ItemsControl AND NOT A ListBox, deliberately, and the scroller does not change
               that: the editor's grammar leaves the arrows to any SelectingItemsControl in the
               focused ancestry, so a selecting list here would swallow the very ⌥↑↓ the grip's
               tooltip advertises. Selection is the view model's own. A ScrollViewer is not a
               SelectingItemsControl and is not one of the surfaces `⌥↑↓` yields to (a text input,
               and nothing else — see KeyboardEditorView.BoardOwnsLayerShortcuts).

               WHY IT IS A `Height` AND NOT A `MaxHeight`. It was bounded in #139 because an
               unbounded list pushed the composer that EDITS the selected row off the bottom of the
               rail. #146 pins it: with a MaxHeight the list is as tall as its contents, so
               recording a step, deleting one or landing on an empty slot slid the composer up or
               down the rail UNDER THE POINTER between one click and the next. A fixed box costs an
               empty gap under a two-step macro and buys a compose bar that never moves.

               The `＋ insert step` row is deliberately OUTSIDE it. It is an action rather than a
               step, and an action that scrolls out of reach is an action the user cannot find. -->
          <ScrollViewer Classes="macroStepList"
                        HorizontalScrollBarVisibility="Disabled"
                        VerticalScrollBarVisibility="Auto">
            <ItemsControl ItemsSource="{Binding Steps.Items}">
              <ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
                <DataTemplate DataType="vm:MacroInspectorStepViewModel">
                  <!-- THE FRAME CARRIES THE SELECTION, not the row button (issue #146). The mock
                       rings the WHOLE row — grip, body and `×` alike — and the outer two sit
                       outside the button, because a button may not nest inside another button.
                       `Border.macroStepRowFrame` is transparent at rest rather than absent, so
                       acquiring the ring costs no reflow and the rows do not shift as the selection
                       moves down the list. -->
                  <Border Classes="macroStepRowFrame" Classes.selected="{Binding IsSelected}">
                    <!-- The row is a Grid rather than one big Button, and the delete action sits
                         BESIDE the selecting button rather than inside it. Two reasons, and both
                         have bitten this codebase: a button nested in a button fires both commands
                         on one click, and a `$parent[...]` binding written inside a themed button's
                         content no longer reaches past the presenter. Every ancestor binding here
                         is therefore anchored on this view, whose DataContext is the panel. -->
                    <Grid ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*,Auto"
                          ColumnSpacing="4"
                          Background="Transparent"
                          Classes.dragSource="{Binding IsDragSource}">
                      <controls:Icon Grid.Column="0"
                                     Classes="dragHandle"
                                     Data="{DynamicResource IconDragHandle}"
                                     ToolTip.Tip="{x:Static vm:MacroInspectorStepsViewModel.ReorderHandleHint}"
                                     VerticalAlignment="Center" />

                      <!-- The row's face is the shared `MacroStepCaption` template, because the
                           drag ghost above the list has to be the same runs in the same faces and a
                           second copy of them would drift from this one. -->
                      <Button Grid.Column="1"
                              Classes="macroStepRow"
                              Command="{Binding $parent[views:MacroInspectorPanelView].((vm:MacroInspectorPanelViewModel)DataContext).Steps.SelectStepCommand}"
                              CommandParameter="{Binding}"
                              Content="{Binding}"
                              ContentTemplate="{StaticResource MacroStepCaption}" />

                      <Button Grid.Column="2"
                              Classes="ghost"
                              Content="×"
                              ToolTip.Tip="{x:Static vm:MacroInspectorStepsViewModel.RemoveStepHint}"
                              Command="{Binding $parent[views:MacroInspectorPanelView].((vm:MacroInspectorPanelViewModel)DataContext).Steps.RemoveStepCommand}"
                              CommandParameter="{Binding}" />

                      <!-- WHERE THE DRAG WOULD LAND. A reorder with no feedback reads as broken
                           even once it works, so the row under a carried step wears a ring while
                           the pointer is over it.

                           A RING, NOT A FILL: the selected row already takes `AccentSelectionFill`,
                           and a drop target painted in it would be indistinguishable from the
                           selection it is about to displace. Last child and
                           `IsHitTestVisible=False`, so it draws over the row's own face without
                           ever becoming the thing the release hit-tests to. -->
                      <Border Grid.Column="0"
                              Grid.ColumnSpan="3"
                              BorderBrush="{DynamicResource AccentBrush}"
                              BorderThickness="1"
                              CornerRadius="{DynamicResource RadiusControl}"
                              IsHitTestVisible="False"
                              IsVisible="{Binding IsDropTarget}" />
                    </Grid>
                  </Border>
                </DataTemplate>
              </ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
            </ItemsControl>
          </ScrollViewer>

          <!-- The trailing row of 2i's list, UNNUMBERED since #146. It opens a placeholder row
               after the selected step — at the end when nothing is selected — and points the
               composer at it, so a step can be inserted in the MIDDLE of a macro. Appending by
               recording is what the header's `● Record sequence` still does. Nothing is written to
               the macro until a key lands on the placeholder. -->
          <Button Classes="macroStepRow" Command="{Binding InsertStepCommand}">
            <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="6" VerticalAlignment="Center">
              <controls:Icon Classes="insertMark" Data="{DynamicResource IconInsert}" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
              <TextBlock Text="{x:Static vm:MacroInspectorStepsViewModel.InsertStepCaption}"
                         Classes="meta"
                         VerticalAlignment="Center" />
            </StackPanel>
          </Button>

          <!-- What the panel refused, and why. Amber: a budget or a full slot strip is a fact about
               the profile, not a failure. -->
          <TextBlock Text="{Binding Message}"
                     Classes="body statusWarning"
                     TextWrapping="Wrap"
                     IsVisible="{Binding HasMessage}" />

          <!-- ===== The compose bar (issues #139, #146) =======================================
               ONE BLOCK THAT EDITS THE SELECTED STEP — its key, the modifiers held with it, what it
               does, and the delay behind it — PINNED DIRECTLY UNDER THE LIST, in the same section,
               so nothing separates the rows from the control that changes them.

               IT IS A BORDERED BOX, and that is what the mock's own title is about: everything
               above it edits the SEQUENCE and everything inside it builds ONE STEP, so the block
               has to read as a tool sitting on the panel rather than as the next paragraph of it.

               IT REPLACED THREE SURFACES. Until #139 the panel could record a run, compose-and-
               APPEND a chord and open a per-row delay editor — and none of the three could change a
               step that already existed.

               ALWAYS VISIBLE, NEVER COLLAPSED, AND DISABLED WITH NOTHING SELECTED. That is a
               deliberate exception to the design's "features a device lacks are not rendered at all
               rather than disabled" (docs/design/README.md), recorded in docs/app/design-system.md
               beside the firmware gate's and the locked position's: nothing here is missing — the
               user simply has not pointed at a step yet — and a block that appeared and vanished as
               rows were clicked would move the whole rail under the pointer on every click. The
               hint under the label is on screen for exactly that state and goes when a step is
               selected.

               NOTHING HAS AN `Apply` AND NOTHING HAS AN `Insert`. Every control writes the moment
               it is touched, through the panel's own `OnMacroWritten` hop, so the session goes
               dirty exactly as it does for a recorded step and there is no half-committed state to
               abandon.

               THE FOUR SECTION CAPTIONS ARE GONE (issue #146). `KEY` / `HELD` / `ACTION` /
               `THEN WAIT` spent four lines of the rail labelling controls the mock leaves unlabelled
               — a latch wearing `⌃` needs no caption saying it is a modifier. `THEN WAIT`'s wording
               survives as the inline `then wait` on row 2, which is the one place the sentence is
               not obvious from the control. -->
          <Border Classes="composerBox">
            <StackPanel Spacing="8">
              <TextBlock Text="{x:Static vm:MacroInspectorPanelViewModel.ComposerLabel}" Classes="sectionLabel" />

              <!-- Said only while every control below is dead, which is the state it exists to
                   explain. With a step selected the sentence would be stale advice taking a line of
                   the rail. -->
              <TextBlock Text="{x:Static vm:MacroInspectorPanelViewModel.ComposerHint}"
                         Classes="meta muted"
                         TextWrapping="Wrap"
                         IsVisible="{Binding !IsComposerEnabled}" />

              <!-- ===== Row 1: the modifiers, the key, and the one-shot Record ================
                   FOUR LATCHES, NOT THE EIGHT #128 OFFERED. Authoring is left-hand only, exactly as
                   the Trigger strip's three are: the right-hand and generic spellings a FILE
                   carries are preserved by Core and drawn by the step row, but nothing new is
                   authored that does not say which side it means. Selecting a step normalizes what
                   it holds to these four, which is the one moment a click is entitled to write.

                   The key field is a READOUT and NOT an `actionField`: that one records a keypress
                   when it is clicked and goes amber while it is armed, and wearing its face here
                   would promise a click that does nothing. Arming is `Record key` beside it, which
                   takes exactly ONE keystroke. #128's reason for existing survives that: `Ctrl+1`
                   is still authorable without pressing it — record the bare `1`, which the window
                   server does deliver, then tick `⌃` here.

                   `Record key` IS `secondary`, NOT `recordAction` (issue #146). The panel already
                   carries one red Record — the header's `Record sequence` — and two would say the
                   sequence and the single step were the same weight of act. Its dot wears
                   `.standby` (muted) while the SingleStep arm is idle and DROPS the class the
                   moment the arm goes live, so red still means "this is recording now" everywhere
                   in the app. `None,Run` is the standby set, because the header's take leaves this
                   button's own arm idle. -->
              <Grid ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*,Auto" ColumnSpacing="8">
                <ItemsControl Grid.Column="0" ItemsSource="{Binding ChordModifiers}" VerticalAlignment="Center">
                  <ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
                    <ItemsPanelTemplate>
                      <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="4" />
                    </ItemsPanelTemplate>
                  </ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
                  <ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
                    <DataTemplate DataType="vm:MacroChordModifier">
                      <Button Classes="macroChip"
                              Classes.selected="{Binding IsOn}"
                              IsEnabled="{Binding IsEnabled}"
                              ToolTip.Tip="{Binding Description}"
                              Command="{Binding $parent[views:MacroInspectorPanelView].((vm:MacroInspectorPanelViewModel)DataContext).ToggleChordModifierCommand}"
                              CommandParameter="{Binding}">
                        <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" VerticalAlignment="Center">
                          <TextBlock Text="{Binding Side}"
                                     Classes="monoValueSmall"
                                     IsVisible="{Binding HasSide}"
                                     VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                          <TextBlock Text="{Binding Symbol}"
                                     Classes="keySymbol"
                                     VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                        </StackPanel>
                      </Button>
                    </DataTemplate>
                  </ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
                </ItemsControl>

                <Border Grid.Column="1" Classes="macroComposerKey">
                  <Grid>
                    <TextBlock Text="{Binding StepTokenText}"
                               Classes="monoValue"
                               Foreground="{DynamicResource MacroStepKeyBrush}"
                               IsVisible="{Binding HasStepKey}"
                               TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis"
                               VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                    <TextBlock Text="{Binding StepTokenText}"
                               Classes="monoValue muted"
                               IsVisible="{Binding !HasStepKey}"
                               VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                  </Grid>
                </Border>

                <Button Grid.Column="2"
                        Classes="secondary"
                        Command="{Binding RecordStepKeyCommand}"
                        VerticalAlignment="Center">
                  <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="6" VerticalAlignment="Center">
                    <Ellipse Classes="recordDot"
                             Classes.standby="{Binding CaptureMode, Converter={StaticResource EnumMatch}, ConverterParameter='None,Run'}"
                             VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                    <!-- The pulse follows THIS arm and not `IsRecording`, which is true for either
                         of the panel's two arms: the header's runs a take until it is stopped, this
                         one takes exactly one keystroke, and two controls pulsing at once would say
                         the panel is capturing twice. -->
                    <TextBlock Text="{Binding RecordStepKeyCaption}"
                               Classes.recording="{Binding CaptureMode, Converter={StaticResource EnumMatch}, ConverterParameter=SingleStep}"
                               VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                  </StackPanel>
                </Button>
              </Grid>

              <!-- ===== Row 2: what the step does, and what it waits =========================
                   `tap` / `press` / `release` — 06 §2.2's three directions, in the step row's own
                   words so the control and the row it edits cannot read differently — then §11.3's
                   `Macro Timing Delays` with its window taken off AND its per-row editor taken
                   away. Every rule the spec pins survives: the tokens are Core's, resolution goes
                   through the TOKEN and never the code, the range is 1-999 with the arrows clamped
                   and a typed value validated, and `FirmwareFeature.CustomMacroDelays` is answered
                   IN PLACE with 09 §2's own words plus an `Update Firmware` button.

                   `composerSegment` AND NOT `macroChip` for these two strips: a chip is an
                   independent latch that RINGS when it is on, and these are genuinely one-of-N
                   choices, which the segment's filled face is what says. Each segment carries its
                   own `IsEnabled`, because `press` and `release` die on a chord: 05 §5.8 discards a
                   direction on a modified keystroke.

                   `none` IS GONE (issue #148). The mock draws `fixed` / `random` and this row now
                   draws what the mock draws; "no delay" is authored by EMPTYING the millisecond
                   field, which is the control the number was going to come from anyway. It is still
                   a state of the step — a step that waits for neither lights neither segment.

                   A GRID, AND DELIBERATELY NOTHING THAT CAN WRAP. This was a WrapPanel precisely
                   because it did not fit: at 440px the row landed on one line or two depending on
                   the font's own metrics, and a compose bar that reflows is not the standing one
                   the mock draws. Three levers made it one line — the `none` segment went, the
                   segments came down to the segmented family's own 11,4 (`ComposerSegment`), and
                   the row grew a column that exists only to be empty.

                   THAT EMPTY `*` IS COLUMN 3, AND IT IS THE ONE THING ON THE ROW THAT MOVES
                   (issue #152). The millisecond field was the `*` itself until then, so a rail
                   dragged past its floor was spent widening a box that holds at most three digits.
                   The slack belongs between `random` and the field instead: the field and its `ms`
                   are then pinned to the row's right edge at every rail width, under `Record key`'s
                   own right edge on the row above, which is how the mock draws the pair. -->
              <StackPanel Spacing="6">
                <Grid ColumnDefinitions="Auto,Auto,Auto,*,Auto,Auto" ColumnSpacing="8">
                  <ItemsControl Grid.Column="0" ItemsSource="{Binding StepDirections}">
                    <ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
                      <ItemsPanelTemplate>
                        <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="4" />
                      </ItemsPanelTemplate>
                    </ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
                    <ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
                      <DataTemplate DataType="vm:MacroStepDirection">
                        <Button Classes="composerSegment"
                                Classes.selected="{Binding IsOn}"
                                IsEnabled="{Binding IsEnabled}"
                                Content="{Binding Caption}"
                                Command="{Binding $parent[views:MacroInspectorPanelView].((vm:MacroInspectorPanelViewModel)DataContext).SetStepDirectionCommand}"
                                CommandParameter="{Binding}" />
                      </DataTemplate>
                    </ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
                  </ItemsControl>

                  <!-- `then wait` is a sentence fragment the controls complete, not a caption over
                       them — which is why it sits between the two strips rather than above either. -->
                  <TextBlock Grid.Column="1"
                             Text="{x:Static vm:MacroInspectorPanelViewModel.StepDelayLabel}"
                             Classes="meta muted"
                             IsVisible="{Binding Steps.AreDelaysAvailable}"
                             VerticalAlignment="Center" />

                  <ItemsControl Grid.Column="2"
                                ItemsSource="{Binding StepDelayOptions}"
                                IsVisible="{Binding Steps.AreDelaysAvailable}">
                    <ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
                      <ItemsPanelTemplate>
                        <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="4" />
                      </ItemsPanelTemplate>
                    </ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
                    <ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
                      <DataTemplate DataType="vm:MacroStepDelayOption">
                        <!-- The tooltip carries §11.3's own caption and the range it accepts.
                             `random`'s 1-150 ms is stated nowhere else: the fixed delay's bounds
                             are drawn by its own caption and its validation message, but a random
                             one has no field. -->
                        <Button Classes="composerSegment"
                                Classes.selected="{Binding IsOn}"
                                IsEnabled="{Binding IsEnabled}"
                                Content="{Binding Caption}"
                                ToolTip.Tip="{Binding Description}"
                                Command="{Binding $parent[views:MacroInspectorPanelView].((vm:MacroInspectorPanelViewModel)DataContext).SetStepDelayModeCommand}"
                                CommandParameter="{Binding}" />
                      </DataTemplate>
                    </ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
                  </ItemsControl>

                  <!-- A real TextBox, and that is correct here: focus landing in one ENDS the
                       recording — the editor's `GotFocus` leg of `StopRecordingOnInteraction`
                       stands the panel down and the banner goes with it — so the digits reach the
                       field and the panel is never left claiming to record while the keyboard
                       belongs to the box (docs/app/keystroke-capture.md, invariant 7). The
                       service's own auto-suspend on text focus still applies underneath, but it
                       is silent, and a silent suspension is exactly what this panel must not rely
                       on.

                       It is the opposite of an action field, which records a KEYPRESS and must
                       never be focusable. Mono — the count is written into the macro verbatim.

                       ENABLED ON THE SECTION AND NOT ON `fixed`, deliberately: typing a valid
                       number is what TAKES the choice off `random`, so a field that went dead
                       whenever `fixed` was not already the answer would be a control with no way of
                       ever becoming reachable. The arrows went with #146 — the segments and the
                       number are the whole control — and since #148 EMPTYING IT IS THE WRITE that
                       takes a delay off, which is what the deleted `none` segment used to do.

                       THREE DIGITS WIDE, AND FIXED (issue #152). §11.3's range is 1-999, so the
                       box never holds a fourth character, and it was the row's `*` column until
                       then — which spent every pixel of a dragged-wider rail on a field that had
                       nothing to put in them. 44 is measured, not chosen: `999` in this face
                       measures 36.4 px inside `MonoValueField`'s 8,6 padding and 1 px border, and
                       the rest is the caret's room and a digit's worth of slack. The empty `*`
                       beside it is what absorbs the rail now, so the pair still gives way last
                       rather than first.

                       `MinWidth` BESIDE THE `Width`, AND IT IS NOT REDUNDANT: FluentTheme's own
                       TextBox carries `TextControlThemeMinWidth` (64), a floor `Width` does not
                       outrank — measure clamps a Width up into MinWidth — so the field silently
                       came back 64 px wide the moment the local 48 that used to hold it down was
                       removed. Both, or neither works.

                       The HEIGHT is the theme's own 32 and stays there: `Border.macroComposerKey`
                       above is pinned to the same step so the readout and this box line up rather
                       than missing by two pixels. -->
                  <TextBox Grid.Column="4"
                           Classes="monoValue"
                           Width="44"
                           MinWidth="44"
                           TextAlignment="Right"
                           IsVisible="{Binding Steps.AreDelaysAvailable}"
                           IsEnabled="{Binding IsStepDelayEnabled}"
                           Text="{Binding StepDelayText, Mode=TwoWay}" />

                  <!-- The unit loses its muted role only while the count is the delay actually in
                       effect. A number typed under `random`, or beside a step that waits for
                       nothing, is a candidate rather than something the file holds, and the mono law
                       is about values that exist verbatim in a config file. -->
                  <TextBlock Grid.Column="5"
                             Text="{x:Static vm:MacroInspectorStepViewModel.MillisecondSuffix}"
                             Classes="meta"
                             Classes.muted="{Binding !IsCustomStepDelay}"
                             IsVisible="{Binding Steps.AreDelaysAvailable}"
                             VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                </Grid>

                <!-- The firmware refusal, drawn in place of the controls rather than beside them: a
                     firmware that cannot do this at all has nothing for the segments to write to.
                     Amber — an old firmware is a fact about the device, not a failure. -->
                <StackPanel Spacing="8" IsVisible="{Binding !Steps.AreDelaysAvailable}">
                  <TextBlock Text="{Binding Steps.DelayUnavailableReason}"
                             Classes="body statusWarning"
                             TextWrapping="Wrap" />
                  <Button Classes="secondary"
                          Content="{Binding Steps.UpdateFirmwareCaption}"
                          Command="{Binding Steps.UpdateFirmwareCommand}"
                          IsVisible="{Binding Steps.CanUpdateFirmware}"
                          HorizontalAlignment="Left" />
                </StackPanel>

                <!-- The panel's ONE error ramp, and it is not an advisory: a millisecond value the
                     user typed outside §11.3's range is a rejected input, where every budget on
                     this panel is a report. -->
                <TextBlock Text="{Binding StepDelayError}"
                           Classes="body statusError"
                           TextWrapping="Wrap"
                           IsVisible="{Binding HasStepDelayError}" />
              </StackPanel>
            </StackPanel>
          </Border>
        </StackPanel>
      </Border>

      <!-- ===== Speed, Repeat and the budgets =================================================
           2i's readings in the mock's own shape: the two things a user SETS, each with the reading
           that counts it on the same line, and the two budgets that are pure report on one muted
           line under them.

           Over budget goes AMBER and still saves — the layout is written as it stands and the board
           truncates. `macros n / m` is 06 §6's profile-wide count; it was the deleted Macros tab's
           footer meter, so this is the only place that limit is readable now, and its maximum comes
           from the device's MacroCapability through MacroLimits — never a literal.

           The two faces are bound EXCLUSIVELY rather than layered, so which one wins is a fact
           about the selector and never about the order the stylesheet happens to declare them in
           (docs/app/design-system.md, contract 7). -->
      <Border Classes="inspectorSection">
        <StackPanel Spacing="8">
          <Grid ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*,Auto" ColumnSpacing="10" IsVisible="{Binding HasSpeed}">
            <TextBlock Grid.Column="0"
                       Text="{Binding SpeedMeter.Label}"
                       Classes="body"
                       VerticalAlignment="Center" />

            <Slider Grid.Column="1"
                    Minimum="{Binding SpeedMinimum}"
                    Maximum="{Binding SpeedMaximum}"
                    Value="{Binding Speed, Mode=TwoWay}"
                    TickFrequency="1"
                    IsSnapToTickEnabled="True"
                    VerticalAlignment="Center" />

            <!-- `5 of 9`, not `5 / 9`: a playback speed is a step out of a scale rather than a
                 consumption against a budget, and the separator is what says so. -->
            <TextBlock Grid.Column="2"
                       Text="{Binding SpeedMeter.Caption}"
                       Classes="monoValue"
                       Classes.statusWarning="{Binding SpeedMeter.IsOverBudget}"
                       VerticalAlignment="Center" />
          </Grid>

          <!-- REPEAT STAYS, as a `− 1 +` stepper. Mockup 2i drew only speed; repeat is supported by
               the devices this app edits today and this panel is the only place left to set it, so
               dropping it would be dropping a working feature silently. The Slider it used to wear
               went with #146: repeat is a small integer range (06 §6) that a slider could neither
               be read exactly on nor stepped by one.

               ABSENT, NEVER DISABLED, where the file does not keep it — the Advantage2 has a repeat
               range in the model but its serializer writes no {xN} token at all. Only the LABEL AND
               THE STEPPER are gated on that, deliberately: the layout keystroke budget beside them
               is a fact about every device that has macros at all, and hiding the whole row would
               take a working readout of a real device limit off the screen with it.

               The captions are the arithmetic signs at the call site rather than icons — both
               embedded families carry them, and a drawn mark would need two more entries in the
               icon catalog to say what `+` and `−` already say. -->
          <Grid ColumnDefinitions="Auto,Auto,*,Auto,Auto" ColumnSpacing="6">
            <TextBlock Grid.Column="0"
                       Text="{x:Static vm:MacroInspectorPanelViewModel.RepeatLabel}"
                       Classes="body"
                       IsVisible="{Binding HasRepeat}"
                       VerticalAlignment="Center" />

            <Border Grid.Column="1" Classes="numericStepper" IsVisible="{Binding HasRepeat}">
              <Grid ColumnDefinitions="Auto,Auto,Auto">
                <Button Grid.Column="0" Classes="ghost" Content="−" Command="{Binding DecreaseRepeatCommand}" />
                <TextBlock Grid.Column="1"
                           Text="{Binding Repeat}"
                           Classes="monoValue"
                           MinWidth="24"
                           TextAlignment="Center"
                           VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                <Button Grid.Column="2" Classes="ghost" Content="+" Command="{Binding IncreaseRepeatCommand}" />
              </Grid>
            </Border>

            <TextBlock Grid.Column="3"
                       Text="{Binding LayoutKeystrokeMeter.Caption}"
                       Classes="monoValue"
                       Classes.muted="{Binding !LayoutKeystrokeMeter.IsOverBudget}"
                       Classes.statusWarning="{Binding LayoutKeystrokeMeter.IsOverBudget}"
                       VerticalAlignment="Center" />

            <TextBlock Grid.Column="4"
                       Text="{x:Static vm:MacroInspectorPanelViewModel.LayoutKeystrokeUnit}"
                       Classes="meta muted"
                       VerticalAlignment="Center" />
          </Grid>

          <!-- `this macro 128 / 500 · macros 24 / 100` WAS DRAWN HERE, and issue #148 deleted it.
               #146 kept the pair as one muted line on the argument that `macros n/m` was the last
               readout of 06 §6's profile-wide count after #140 removed the Macros tab. The mock
               draws neither, the user chose the mock knowing exactly that, and the choice is
               recorded in docs/app/design-system.md rather than absorbed: the profile-wide macro
               count and the per-macro cap are no longer stated anywhere in the app.

               `LayoutKeystrokeMeter` above STAYS — the mock draws that one, on the Repeat row. -->
        </StackPanel>
      </Border>

      <!-- ===== The two macro actions =========================================================
           WHAT THE DELETED MACROS TAB DID TO A WHOLE NAMED GROUP, done to ONE macro on ONE key
           (issue #141). `Copy macro to…` arms the editor's own pick — the same arm/cancel pair the
           legend row's `Copy key…` and the rail footer's `Copy to…` use, so there is one armed
           state, one Escape route and one disarm set — and the next cap clicked receives a CLONE in
           its first empty slot. `Delete` empties this slot alone; nothing else on the key and no
           other key moves, because there is no shared macro to reach (06 §1).

           THE NOUN IS LOAD-BEARING: the rail's own footer, a few rows below this one, carries a
           `Copy to…` that copies the WHOLE position. Two buttons reading `Copy to…` on one rail
           would be two different actions with one name — which is why this one keeps its noun even
           though the mock shortens it.

           THE CANCEL TAKES THE SAME SLOT, exactly as the rail footer's does: the button that armed
           the pick is what ends it, so the panel never grows a dead control it only sometimes
           needs.

           `Delete` IS `discard` — THE RED FACE — SINCE #146, and that is a deliberate widening of a
           face this app had reserved for the destructive ANSWER of a message box. The designer drew
           it red, and the reason survives the move: emptying a slot is the one action on this rail
           that destroys something the file holds. Recorded in docs/app/design-system.md. -->
      <Border Classes="inspectorSection">
        <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="8" HorizontalAlignment="Right">
          <Button Classes="secondary"
                  Content="{x:Static vm:MacroInspectorPanelViewModel.CopyMacroCaption}"
                  Command="{Binding CopyMacroCommand}"
                  IsVisible="{Binding !IsCopyArmed}" />
          <Button Classes="secondary"
                  Content="{x:Static vm:MacroInspectorPanelViewModel.CancelCopyCaption}"
                  Command="{Binding CancelCopyCommand}"
                  IsVisible="{Binding IsCopyArmed}" />
          <Button Classes="discard"
                  Content="{x:Static vm:MacroInspectorPanelViewModel.DeleteMacroCaption}"
                  Command="{Binding DeleteMacroCommand}" />
        </StackPanel>
      </Border>
    </StackPanel>
    </StackPanel>

    <!-- ===== The carried step ================================================================
         WHAT IS BEING DRAGGED, not only where it would land (issue #128). The drop ring alone left
         the gesture's subject invisible — the list did not move until the pointer came up, which is
         exactly the "the view is only updated after the movement" the user reported. A copy of the
         row now rides the pointer from the 4 px threshold until the gesture ends.

         `IsHitTestVisible="False"` IS MANDATORY, on the layer and on the ghost alike. The drop row
         is resolved by hit-testing the release position against the live tree, so a ghost that
         could be hit would sit under the pointer and answer every one of those tests itself — the
         drag would land on nothing, every time.

         Its content is the row's own `MacroStepCaption`, delay and all, and its position is written
         straight from `OnPointerMovedOverStep`; a Canvas measures to nothing, so this costs the
         panel above it no height at all. -->
    <Canvas x:Name="DragLayer" IsHitTestVisible="False">
      <Border x:Name="DragGhost"
              Classes="macroStepGhost"
              IsHitTestVisible="False"
              IsVisible="False">
        <ContentControl x:Name="DragGhostContent" ContentTemplate="{StaticResource MacroStepCaption}" />
      </Border>
    </Canvas>
  </Grid>
</UserControl>
